<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xu Shen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Jilin University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Song Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Central Florida
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhen Tan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Arizona State University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Laura Yao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xinyu Zhao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kaidi Xu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>City University of Hong Kong
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xin Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Jilin University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tianlong Chen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting to improve problem-solving and provide seemingly transparent explanations. However, growing evidence shows that CoT often fail to faithfully represent the underlying reasoning process, raising concerns about their reliability in high-risk applications. Although prior studies have focused on mechanism-level analyses showing that CoTs can be unfaithful, they leave open the practical challenge of deciding whether a specific trajectory is faithful to the internal reasoning of the model. To address this gap, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>, a unified benchmark for <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance-level CoT unfaithfulness detection</span>. Our framework establishes a rigorous task formulation that formulates unfaithfulness detection as a discriminative decision problem, and provides <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">F</span>aithfulness <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">IN</span>stance <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">E</span>valuation for Chain-of-Thought), an expert-annotated collection of over 1,000 trajectories generated by four representative LLMs across four domains, including more than 300 unfaithful instances with fine-grained causes and step-level evidence. We further conduct a systematic evaluation of eleven representative detection methods spanning counterfactual, logit-based, and LLM-as-judge paradigms, deriving empirical insights that clarify the strengths and weaknesses of existing approaches and reveal the increased challenges of detection in knowledge-intensive domains and with more advanced models.
To the best of our knowledge, <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> establishes the first comprehensive benchmark for instance-level CoT faithfulness, setting a solid basis for future research toward more interpretable and trustworthy reasoning in LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance across domains such as mathematics, programming, logic and commonsense reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Imani et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang &amp; Chang, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
A key driver of this progress is <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chain-of-Thought</span> (CoT) prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which elicits step-by-step reasoning traces that not only improve problem-solving but also give the appearance of transparency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Besta et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, leading to their increasing use in high-stakes domains such as medicine and law where accountability and reliability are critical <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">ShengbinYue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, an increasing number of studies have questioned the supposed transparency and interpretability brought by CoT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, it becomes critical to ask: <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">do these reasoning steps faithfully represent the model’s internal decision process, or are they merely surface-level accounts designed to appear aligned with the instruction?</span>
Addressing this question is essential for rigorously assessing the interpretability and trustworthiness of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An increasing number of studies have examined the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">faithfulness of CoT</span>, a key concept in interpretability that assesses whether an explanation aligns with the model’s actual reasoning or decision-making process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanham et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
Existing approaches can be broadly grouped into three categories:
➀ <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Couterfactual-based methods.</span>
These approaches probe CoT faithfulness by perturbing reasoning traces, for example by adding mistakes, removing steps, or forcing early answers, and then testing whether the final prediction changes accordingly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paul et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yee et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Such causal sensitivity is treated as evidence of faithfulness, since a faithful CoT should preserve a causal link between intermediate steps and the final answer.
➁ <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logit-based methods.</span>
These studies leverage model-internal diagnostics, such as analyzing logit variations or measuring information gain to approximate causal alignment between CoTs and outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tanneru et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
➂ <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-Judge methods.</span>
These methods rely on stronger language models to directly evaluate the quality of reasoning traces, using carefully designed prompts to assess whether a CoT reflects genuine reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arcuschin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Despite differences in methodology, these studies converge on a consistent finding: <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">CoT explanations often fail to faithfully capture the underlying reasoning process of LLMs.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04040v1/pipelinefull.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/197;" width="548" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of <span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>. The framework unifies task formulation, dataset construction, and systematic evaluation for instance-level unfaithful CoT detection. We collect CoT traces from four domains and four LLMs, annotate them through a multi-stage human pipeline to build the <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> dataset, and benchmark existing detection methods across counterfactual, logit-based, and LLM-as-Judge paradigms.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these findings, most existing studies on CoT faithfulness focus on probing the mechanism itself, and thus provide only aggregate evidence that CoTs may be unfaithful.
Such analyses, while valuable for understanding the limitations of the CoT paradigm, do not resolve the practical challenge faced by end users: <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">given a specific query and a produced CoT, can we determine whether this particular reasoning trace is unfaithful?</span> To better frame this challenge, we highlight three open questions. First, <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what</span> constitutes a practically meaningful task for evaluating CoT faithfulness remains unclear, since current research lacks a rigorous task formulation that treats unfaithfulness detection as an instance-level discriminative problem. Second, <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">why</span> a CoT should be judged unfaithful is rarely investigated, as there is no dataset with expert-verified ground truth that systematically captures diverse unfaithfulness patterns across domains and models. Third, <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> faithfulness should be evaluated remains unsettled, since existing methods use heterogeneous criteria and often conflate faithfulness with common metrics such as correctness or answer accuracy. These gaps underscore the need for a systematic benchmark that provides a clear task definition, reliable data, and rigorous evaluation of existing methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To fill these gaps, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>, a unified benchmark for studying <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance-level CoT unfaithfulness detection</span>. <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> integrates three complementary components. First, it formalizes the task of <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance-level CoT unfaithfulness detection</span>, providing a rigorous discriminative formulation that decides, given a query and its reasoning trajectory, whether the trajectory faithfully reflects the model’s internal decision process. Second, it introduces <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> (<span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">F</span>aithfulness <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">IN</span>stance <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">E</span>valuation for Chain-of-Thought), a dedicated dataset built from CoT trajectories generated by four representative LLMs on tasks from four domains. We collect over <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,000</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories and annotate them through expert evaluation, guided by two core reasons of unfaithfulness further refined into eight principles. Each instance is labeled with unfaithfulness status, reasons for unfaithfulness, and step-level evidence.
Third, <span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> establishes a systematic evaluation of eleven detection methods across counterfactual, logit-based, and LLM-as-judge paradigms, yielding new empirical insights into the strengths and weaknesses of current approaches. An overview of the framework is shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To the best of our knowledge, <span id="S1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> is the first comprehensive foundation for rigorous, instance-level evaluation of CoT unfaithfulness.
To sum up, the main contributions of this paper are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unified benchmark.</span> We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>, which integrates a precise task formulation, a dedicated dataset, and a systematic evaluation protocol into a single unified framework. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first comprehensive benchmark that enables rigorous instance-level study of CoT unfaithfulness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert-annotated dataset.</span> As part of <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>, we construct <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span>, a dataset of over <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,000</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning trajectories generated by four representative LLMs across four domains. Each trajectory is annotated by experts with labels, fine-grained causes of unfaithfulness, and step-level evidence, enabling principled and fine-grained analysis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Systematic evaluation.</span> We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> representative detection methods, spanning counterfactual, logit-based, and LLM-as-Judge, and derive key empirical insights into their strengths, limitations, and domain-specific challenges.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem Formulation: Instance-level CoT Unfaithfulness Detection</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Background</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning refers to the practice of generating intermediate steps before arriving at a final answer, with the aim of making a model’s reasoning process more explicit and interpretable. Formally, given a query <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and a prompt <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">let’s think step by step</span>), a large language model <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{M}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a reasoning trajectory <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=(c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=(c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> with a length of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, which then yields a final answer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. In many applications, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as an explanation of how <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> was obtained.
Despite providing explicit intermediate steps, CoT often produces reasoning traces that appear coherent yet fail to reflect the model’s actual decision process. This limitation motivates instance-level evaluation of whether a given <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is faithful to the model’s internal reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Formal Definition</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most existing studies on CoT faithfulness focus on analyzing the mechanism itself, often through diagnostic tests such as counterfactual interventions, early-answer forcing, or logit-based analyses. These approaches typically assess whether the CoT paradigm as a whole can fail, thereby offering population-level evidence that CoTs may be unfaithful. However, such findings do not answer the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">instance-level question</span>: given a specific query, prompt, and a produced CoT, does this particular reasoning trace faithfully reflect the model’s underlying reasoning? Addressing this instance-level detection problem is crucial for practical deployment, since end users interact with individual explanations rather than aggregate statistics. To fill this gap, we provide a precise formulation and to our knowledge this is the first explicit treatment of CoT faithfulness as a discriminative task. In particular, we cast it as a binary classification problem that decides faithful versus unfaithful for an observed CoT. We next present the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Instance-level CoT Unfaithfulness Detection</em>, which forms the foundation for our subsequent analysis and dataset construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Instance-level CoT Unfaithfulness Detection)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the query <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and a generated chain-of-thought (CoT) trajectory
<math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=(c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=(c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> produced by a large language model <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the task of unfaithfulness detection is to decide whether <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> faithfully reflects the internal reasoning process of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be written as <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, this is defined as a binary classification function</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(q,\mathcal{C})\mapsto\{0,1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(q,\mathcal{C})\mapsto\{0,1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(q,\mathcal{C})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(q,\mathcal{C})=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is unfaithful and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(q,\mathcal{C})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(q,\mathcal{C})=0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that it is faithful.
Different detection algorithms instantiate <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in different ways.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Challenges.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the above definition provides a formal criterion for faithfulness detection, evaluating CoT faithfulness at the instance level remains inherently difficult because large language models operate as black-box systems.
This opacity makes it fundamentally unclear how to determine whether a generated CoT truly reflects the model’s internal reasoning.
From this perspective, two central challenges emerge:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">➀<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lack of ground truth.</span> The internal reasoning path <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is unobservable, meaning that there is no direct ground truth available for calibration or supervision. This makes it impossible to verify unfaithfulness in a straightforward manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">➁<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lack of effective evaluation methods.</span> Since <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be observed, the definition of faithfulness necessarily relies on an abstract alignment between the observed CoT and the latent reasoning process. Yet it remains unclear whether existing methods can operationalize this alignment to reliably detect unfaithfulness at the instance level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these challenges suggest that the core obstacle in assessing instance-level CoT faithfulness lies not only in the absence of ground-truth annotations of unfaithful reasoning, but also in the uncertainty surrounding how to evaluate existing methods, highlighting the need for dedicated datasets and comprehensive benchmarking.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT</h2>

<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h5>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><img src="2510.04040v1/two_reason.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/165;" width="274" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Two primary reasons of unfaithfulness</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The challenges outlined above raise a natural question: <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">if the internal reasoning path <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is fundamentally unobservable, how can we meaningfully label CoTs as faithful or unfaithful at the instance level?</span> Insights from prior work in natural language processing and cognitive science suggest that unfaithfulness often leaves observable traces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">McKaughan &amp; Howard-Snyder, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacovi &amp; Goldberg, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In practice, such cases tend to expose subtle clues that distinguish them from genuinely faithful reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cousins et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Consequently, unfaithful CoTs often display recognizable patterns that set them apart from faithful reasoning. Building on this insight, we focus on observable signals of unfaithfulness as practical substitutes for the inaccessible ground-truth reasoning path. Leveraging these signals, we construct the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> through expert human annotation, where each CoT trajectory is labeled for faithfulness and further categorized into fine-grained failure types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Two Reasons for Unfaithful CoT.</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through a synthesis of prior work on CoT mechanisms and unfaithful explanations, we identify two primary reasons of unfaithfulness: <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Post-hoc Reasoning</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spurious Reasoning Chains</span> (see Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Motivation. ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The first refers to cases where a reasoning trajectory is constructed after the fact to justify a predetermined answer, while the second reflects breaks in the causal or logical continuity of the reasoning process. Together, these two categories provide a principled taxonomy that guides our annotation and offers a systematic perspective on unfaithful CoTs. We now give the definitions of these two reasons.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Post-hoc Reasoning)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A CoT exhibits post-hoc reasoning when its intermediate steps are retroactively constructed to rationalize a predetermined answer, rather than reflecting the model’s actual causal decision process.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While post-hoc reasoning emphasizes the tendency of models to rationalize decisions <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after the fact</span>,
a second type of unfaithfulness arises when the reasoning process itself breaks down.
In these cases, the trajectory does not simply rationalize an answer incorrectly,
but instead fails to maintain internal logical continuity, leaving gaps, contradictions, or irrelevant reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Spurious Reasoning Chains)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A CoT exhibits a spurious reasoning chain when its steps appear superficially coherent but fail to maintain a genuine causal connection to the question or to the final answer.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these two categories capture different sources of unfaithful reasoning:
one comes from retroactive rationalization, the other from breaks in the reasoning process.
To our knowledge, this is the first clear summary of the main causes of unfaithful CoTs,
which serves as a basis for consistent annotation and further analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Building the <span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> Dataset</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second component of <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> is <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span>, an expert-annotated resource for instance-level CoT faithfulness.
It follows the two-category taxonomy to systematically identify unfaithful traces across domains, providing standardized ground truth for evaluation.
Details of its collection and annotation are presented below, with additional information in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Dataset and Model Setup ‣ Appendix A Additional Details of FaithCoT-Bench ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Instance Setting.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each instance in <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> dataset is designed to capture the essential elements needed for evaluating CoT faithfulness in a controlled yet diverse setting.
Every instance consists of three components.
➀ <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Query:</span> A question sampled from one of four widely used benchmarks, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LogicQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AuQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ling et al. (2017)</a></cite>, and the biomedical portion of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HLE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Phan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which collectively span four domains: logic, factual reasoning, mathematics, and biology.
This selection ensures coverage of heterogeneous reasoning challenges and avoids overfitting to a single type of task.
➁ <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generated CoT and Answer:</span> For each query, we include a chain-of-thought trajectory and its corresponding final answer, generated by one of four representative large language models.
To elicit CoTs in a controlled and comparable manner, we employ a standardized prompting strategy, with prompt templates provided in Appendix <a href="#Ax1.SS4" title="A.4 Prompts for the Large language model ‣ Appendix A Additional Details of FaithCoT-Bench ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.
We select two recent open-source models (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA3.1-8B</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>) and two closed-source models (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 2.5 Flash</span>).
This design allows us to probe faithfulness not only across different reasoning domains but also across different model families and training paradigms.
➂ <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Annotations:</span> Each instance is paired with human-provided labels that determine whether the CoT is faithful.
For unfaithful cases, annotators further specify the primary reason (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">post-hoc reasoning</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spurious reasoning chain</span>) and, when applicable, the precise step(s) most responsible for the breakdown, supplemented with a short natural-language explanation.
Together, these elements enable fine-grained analysis of whether a model’s reasoning trace genuinely reflects its internal computation process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human Annotation Procedure.</h5>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04040v1/comparison_plot.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/114;" width="274" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Human Annotation’s Kappa.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure reliable annotations, we adopt a multi-round procedure conducted by two domain experts in LLM reasoning.
Both annotators follow a standardized guideline (see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 annotator instruction sheet ‣ Appendix A Additional Details of FaithCoT-Bench ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) that operationalizes our taxonomy into clear criteria.
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Round I:</span> Each annotator independently labels every CoT on three aspects: (i) whether it is faithful, (ii) confidence level, and (iii) if unfaithful, the reason and key step(s) responsible.
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Round II:</span> Cases with low confidence or disagreement are resolved through collaborative discussion, with persuasion and justification prioritized over majority voting.
During this stage, the two macro categories of unfaithfulness are further refined into a set of fine-grained subtypes derived from recurring annotation patterns.
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Round III:</span> Annotators cross-check each other’s labels against the refined taxonomy, re-examining disagreements until consensus is reached; unresolved cases are discarded.
This process enforces consistency, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Human Annotation Procedure. ‣ 3.2 Building the FINE-CoT Dataset ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the final inter-annotator agreement remains high across all domains, with Cohen’s Kappa ranging from 81.0 to 97.2, underscoring the reliability and trustworthiness of our ground-truth labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Basic Statistics of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> dataset</h5>

<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04040v1/Unfaithful_Ratio.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/114;" width="274" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Statistics on the unfaithfulness ratio.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In total, the constructed <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> contains over 300 annotated unfaithful CoTs.
Each annotated instance includes not only a binary label of faithfulness but also the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unfaithful reason</span> and the specific <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">steps</span> responsible for unfaithfulness. We provide illustrative examples in Appendix <a href="#Ax3.SS5" title="A.5 unfaithful cot example from FINE-CoT ‣ Appendix A Additional Details of FaithCoT-Bench ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>
The statistics of unfaithful ratios across models and domains are presented in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Basic Statistics of FINE-CoT dataset ‣ 3.2 Building the FINE-CoT Dataset ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
revealing substantial variation, with higher rates observed in knowledge-intensive datasets such as <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BLE-Bio</span>. More detailed statistical analyses are provided in later sections.
Among all unfaithful instances, 41.66% are categorized as <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">post-hoc reasoning</span>, while 57.71% fall into <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spurious reasoning chains</span>,
which are further refined into eight fine-grained principles introduced in the following subsection. For CoTs judged to be faithful, we additionally record a confidence score from the annotators, which may serve as a useful signal for future studies, for example in filtering uncertain cases or calibrating automatic detectors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-grained Principles.</h5>

<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><img src="2510.04040v1/eight_reason.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/130;" width="274" height="130" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Eight Fine-grained principles of unfaithfulness in CoT</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the two high-level causes of unfaithfulness, we further refine them into eight fine-grained signals, four under <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">post-hoc reasoning</span> and four under <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spurious reasoning chains</span> (see Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Fine-grained Principles. ‣ 3.2 Building the FINE-CoT Dataset ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Fine-grained Principles. ‣ 3.2 Building the FINE-CoT Dataset ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the most frequent signal is step skipping (24.36%), followed by selective explanation bias (19.74%), while other categories such as weak justification or post-answer analysis occur less often, highlighting both dominant and subtle failure modes.
These signals capture common surface patterns such as selective explanation bias or step skipping, which provide concrete operational markers of how unfaithfulness manifests in practice.
Importantly, the signals are not limited to guiding our current annotation: they also establish reusable criteria for future dataset construction and offer researchers a principled basis to analyze why and how unfaithful CoTs arise.
Detailed descriptions, statistical results, and representative examples of these eight signals are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Fine-grained Definitions of Unfaithful CoT Signals ‣ Appendix A Additional Details of FaithCoT-Bench ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Empirical Observations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the distribution of unfaithful instances in the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> dataset, producing empirical observations that reveal how unfaithfulness manifests across tasks, domains, and model families.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❶:</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoT faithfulness does not align perfectly with task-level accuracy.</span>
Our analysis shows that higher accuracy on benchmark tasks does not necessarily imply more faithful CoT reasoning.
For instance, on the <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AQuA</span> dataset, <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> attains an accuracy of 88.6%, exceeding <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA3.1-8B</span> (75.3%).
However, <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> also produces a higher proportion of unfaithful CoTs (26.0% vs. 22.0%). Detailed results are presented in Figure <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Materials for Observations ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
This discrepancy arises because current LLM training primarily optimizes for correct answers, with little emphasis on ensuring that the reasoning trace faithfully reflects the internal decision process.
We argue that unfaithfulness in CoTs should be explicitly reported as a complementary evaluation dimension in future LLM releases: <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">beyond accuracy, models must be assessed on whether their explanations faithfully represent their reasoning.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❷:</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">At the instance level, correctness and faithfulness diverge.</span>
Across all datasets and models, we observe 189 <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong-faithful</span>, 204 <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong-unfaithful</span>, 605 <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct-faithful</span>, and 185 <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct-unfaithful</span> cases (see Figure <a href="#S3.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 3.3 Empirical Observations ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6a</span></a>).
Although the largest category is <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct-faithful</span>, the remaining three categories together account for nearly 40% of all instances. This means that a correct answer is not sufficient evidence of faithful reasoning, and conversely, even incorrect answers can expose reasoning in a transparent and faithful manner.
We argue that <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">faithfulness should be explicitly reported as a complementary evaluation dimension for future LLM releases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❸:</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Faithfulness varies systematically across models.</span>
When comparing distributions by model, <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini-2.5 flash</span> produce the highest proportion of <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct-faithful</span> traces (182 and 206 cases, respectively), while <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA3.1-8B</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> exhibit higher rates of unfaithfulness, particularly in <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong-unfaithful</span> cases (78 for the first, 63 for the second).
This suggests that larger or more instruction-tuned models may not only yield higher accuracy but also improve reasoning transparency. However, the persistence of over 15–25% unfaithful traces even in the strongest models highlights the limits of scale alone in solving the faithfulness problem. Specific results are provided in Figure <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Materials for Observations ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>
Thus, <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">improving model size or alignment techniques can mitigate but not eliminate unfaithful reasoning, underscoring the need for explicit faithfulness-oriented objectives.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❹:</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task type strongly influences faithfulness.</span>
Breaking down results by dataset, we find that reasoning-heavy domains like <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AQuA</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LogicQA</span> contain substantial numbers of <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong-faithful</span> cases (11 and 73, respectively), reflecting that models are often transparent even when mistaken in logical or mathematical reasoning.
By contrast, knowledge-intensive tasks like <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BLE-bio</span> show higher proportions of <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrong-unfaithful</span> cases (79 and 44, respectively), indicating that models tend to fabricate plausible but misleading explanations when lacking domain knowledge.
Through further statistical analyses, we confirm that symbolic reasoning formats such as logic or mathematics are more likely to yield faithful CoTs, whereas factual and domain-specific tasks more frequently trigger unfaithful reasoning. All the statistical evidence can be found in Figure <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Materials for Observations ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
We conclude that <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">faithfulness is shaped not only by model capability but also by task demands, and evaluation must therefore be domain-sensitive.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04040v1/four_type_stat.svg" id="S3.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:187/114;" width="187" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Distribution of CoT Faithfulness vs Correctness</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04040v1/hardness_stat.svg" id="S3.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/114;" width="176" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Unfaithfulness rates increase with task difficulty</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04040v1/ood_stat.svg" id="S3.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/114;" width="176" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Unfaithfulness amplified under distribution shift</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Systematic analysis of unfaithful CoTs from different statistical perspectives.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❺:</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem difficulty and distribution shift are key drivers of unfaithful CoTs.</span>
Our analysis shows that unfaithfulness increases not only with intrinsic problem complexity but also under distributional shift. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 3.3 Empirical Observations ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6b</span></a>, both very easy and very hard problems yield higher unfaithful rates (<span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LogiQA</span>: 18.18% for easy vs. 38.25% for hard), albeit for different reasons: trivial problems invite post-hoc rationalizations, whereas difficult ones induce step-skipping or contradictions. Beyond difficulty, Figure <a href="#S3.F6.sf3" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 3.3 Empirical Observations ‣ 3 FINE-CoT Dataset: Ground Truth for Unfaithful CoT ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6c</span></a> further shows sharp rises in unfaithfulness when moving from in-distribution to out-of-distribution settings (<span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HLE-Bio</span>: 20.22% ID vs. 73.91% OOD). Taken together, these findings suggest that <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">when problems are either very difficult or fall into out-of-distribution scenarios, CoTs are especially prone to unfaithful reasoning.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Benchmarking Methods for CoT Unfaithfulness Detection</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to the third component of <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>:
a systematic evaluation of existing methods.
Whereas prior work has mainly provided aggregate analyses of CoT unfaithfulness,
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> enables instance-level comparison across models and tasks.
It provides a unified reference for fair assessment of counterfactual, logit-based, and LLM-as-judge approaches,
and allows us to uncover their respective strengths and limitations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Evaluation Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark eleven methods for CoT faithfulness detection, spanning four paradigms:
➀ <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines:</span> A random classifier defines the lower bound, while perplexity-based scoring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uses sentence fluency as a proxy.
➁ <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Counterfactual-based:</span> Methods such as Adding Mistakes, Option Shuffling, Removing Steps, Early Answering, and Paraphrasing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanham et al., 2023</a>)</cite> test whether perturbations to the CoT alter the answer, thereby evaluating unfaithfulness.
➂ <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logit-based:</span> Approaches like Answer Tracing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and Information Gain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> analyze token-level logits to capture unfaithfulness signals.
➃ <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-Judge:</span> Step-Judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> inspects individual steps, while Faithful-Judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arcuschin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> evaluates the overall trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Method performance is assessed using three complementary metrics: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math></span>, measuring agreement with human annotations beyond chance; (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span>, capturing overall classification correctness; and (3) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1</span>, balancing precision and recall under class imbalance. For clarity, Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Evaluation Setup ‣ 4 Benchmarking Methods for CoT Unfaithfulness Detection ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports only F1 scores as the primary comparison metric. The complete results table and detailed descriptions of all evaluated methods are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C ADDITIONAL EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
