</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Empirical Observations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having benchmarked existing methods on <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> dataset, we next examine the empirical results.
Our analysis uncovers consistent patterns in when current approaches succeed or fail at detecting unfaithful reasoning,
highlighting key limitations of existing methods and offering insights into the conditions under which CoT faithfulness can be more reliably assessed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❶:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-judge methods consistently outperform alternatives, while logit-based methods perform the worst.</span>
Across all datasets and models, LLM-as-judge methods achieve the highest F1 scores, typically in the range of 65 to 77 and on average exceeding other paradigms by more than <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that rubric-driven evaluation is effective for capturing subtle unfaithfulness such as post-hoc rationalization and spurious reasoning chains.
Nevertheless, performance remains below ideal, with notable drops in knowledge-intensive settings, reflecting sensitivity to rubric design and textual subjectivity.
In contrast, logit-based methods such as Answer Tracing and Information Gain often score below 50 and sometimes under 20, indicating that token-level probability signals alone are insufficient to distinguish faithful from unfaithful reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❷:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Counterfactual-based methods are effective only when interventions target causally critical steps.</span>
These methods perturb reasoning steps and check whether the final answer changes, which is informative only if the modified step is causally important.
Accordingly, performance varies across domains: in mathematics tasks such as <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AQuA</span>, where reasoning chains are tightly causal, Adding Mistakes achieves strong F1 scores (e.g., 66.7 on <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA3.1-8B</span> and 59.2 on <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span>).
By contrast, in knowledge-intensive tasks like <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> or <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HLE-Bio</span>, interventions often affect peripheral steps and leave the answer unchanged, leading to weak results.
Thus, counterfactual approaches are useful diagnostics in domains with dense causal chains but unreliable when reasoning contains redundancy or loose connections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❸:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoT reasoning errors do not necessarily imply unfaithfulness.</span>
A CoT may contain incorrect reasoning steps yet remain faithful if it truthfully exposes the model’s internal process.
This distinction is evident in our benchmark results: Step-Judge, which penalizes step-level errors, consistently performs worse than Faithful-Judge (e.g., 69.2 vs. 79.2 on <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HLE-Bio</span>, 38.8 vs. 50.9 on <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span>).
These gaps confirm that equating reasoning errors with unfaithfulness conflates correctness and faithfulness, and highlight the need for evaluation methods that target transparency rather than factual accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❹:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Faithfulness detection is more difficult in knowledge-intensive domains.</span>
Across all methods, performance on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HLE-Bio</span> is consistently lower than on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LogicQA</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AQuA</span>.
For example, on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> with <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-8B</span>, most counterfactual-based methods remain in the 30–50 range, while logit-based methods are only modest (e.g., 45.5 for Answer Tracing).
Similarly, in <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HLE-Bio</span>, logit-based scores drop sharply, with Information Gain as low as 9.5.
These results indicate that detecting unfaithful reasoning in knowledge-intensive domains is inherently more difficult, as subtle reasoning flaws are less obvious and harder to judge, even for human annotators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Observation ❺:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stronger models do not guarantee easier faithfulness detection.</span>
While <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini2.5-flash</span> represent more advanced model families, their outputs do not consistently lead to higher detection scores compared to smaller open-source models.
For instance, Faithful-Judge performs well on <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TruthfulQA</span> with <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B</span> (76.1) but drops notably with <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o-mini</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini2.5-flash</span> (around 50). This counterintuitive trend suggests that larger models, though generally more accurate, also produce more sophisticated but misleading CoTs, making unfaithfulness harder to detect.
The result highlights a scalability paradox: as LLMs improve in fluency and reasoning coverage, their explanations become more deceptively plausible, raising the bar for faithful evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Understanding Chain-of-Thought Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting has become a widely adopted approach for enhancing the reasoning abilities of large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Besta et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
By eliciting intermediate reasoning steps, CoT improves accuracy on multi-step tasks ranging from mathematics and logic to commonsense and scientific question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Imani et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang &amp; Chang, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and has inspired numerous extensions that restructure or enrich the reasoning process.
More recently, high-quality CoTs have been leveraged as supervision signals, either for reinforcement learning to strengthen reasoning skills or for distillation to transfer reasoning ability into smaller models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stechly et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Taken together, CoT has evolved from a simple prompting technique into a paradigm central to both reasoning performance and interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, making it a natural entry point for examining whether its explanations are truly faithful to the model’s decision process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Explainability and Faithfulness in CoT</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CoTs are increasingly treated as explanations, with their step-by-step traces often assumed to enhance model interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
They have already been applied in high-stakes domains such as medicine, law, and finance, where transparent reasoning is crucial for trust and accountability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">ShengbinYue et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
However, recent studies reveal that CoTs are often unfaithful: they may contain factual mistakes, incoherent steps, or post-hoc rationalizations that obscure the true reasoning process<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Existing approaches to evaluating faithfulness include Counterfactual-based methods, yet most of these analyses remain at an aggregate or mechanism level, leaving open the question of how to assess unfaithfulness at the level of individual instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paul et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yee et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Our work addresses this gap by introducing the first benchmark dedicated to instance-level CoT unfaithfulness evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we presented <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span>, the first unified benchmark for instance-level CoT faithfulness detection.
By defining a rigorous task formulation, introducing an expert-annotated dataset <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span>, and systematically evaluating representative methods, our study provides both a resource and empirical insights into the limitations of current approaches.
Our findings show that unfaithfulness is widespread, especially in knowledge-intensive domains and with stronger models, and that existing methods struggle to deliver consistent reliability.
We expect <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> to advance the development of more faithful, interpretable, and trustworthy reasoning in LLMs, ultimately supporting safer and more reliable deployment in real-world applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study is confined to purely scientific questions, without the involvement of human participants, animals, or environmentally sensitive materials. As such, it presents no ethical risks or conflicts of interest. We uphold the highest standards of scientific integrity and ethics to ensure the validity and reliability of our findings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All components of <span id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FaithCoT-Bench</span> are designed to ensure reproducibility. The benchmark task is formally defined in the main text. Detailed descriptions of <span id="Sx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FINE-CoT</span> dataset and annotation protocols are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional Details of FaithCoT-Bench ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, supplementary analyses of empirical observations are included in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Supplementary Materials for Observations ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, and additional methodological details together with the complete experimental results are presented in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C ADDITIONAL EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS ‣ FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. All results are reported under standardized evaluation criteria, and baselines have been rigorously cross-checked to ensure fairness and consistency.The code are provide in
<a href="https://github.com/se7esx/FaithCoT-BENCH" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/se7esx/FaithCoT-BENCH</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
